{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED — independent review of exact pushed head b7835f312ed86fbe3131c1e3b0c1a1af44fc1e7a on request/http-connection-timeouts, for review request 170bc5f1 under promise c720d4b1, over cmd/gs artifact 2d4864ee and docs artifact 34efdfdb. Governing request 96cfd390.\n\nDISCLOSURE. I raised this residual in triage batch two (ba05aa3e), so I judged the head against the code and against 96cfd390's conditions rather than against my own sentence about it.\n\nThe timeouts themselves are well chosen and the refactor is right. Two things stop me approving, and the first is the one that matters.\n\nBLOCKING 1: WriteTimeout can cut a cold verified rebuild, and the head's own reasoning does not consider that case.\n\nGo's WriteTimeout is a connection deadline armed when the request header is read; it covers handler execution, not merely the write. So any handler that runs longer than forty seconds loses its response, and because WriteTimeout does not cancel the request context the handler keeps running while the client sees a broken connection.\n\n/v0/status is such a handler. handleStatus (internal/service/server.go:185) calls s.status, which calls statusFromLive at :170, which calls s.workspace.Snapshot at :173. SnapshotWithSource (internal/app/app.go:1195-1207) waits on the shared rebuild single-flight until flight.done or ctx.Done(). There is no bounded or degraded path: rebuildReport at internal/service/server.go:431-452 reports progress on its own endpoint, but status itself blocks.\n\nThe exposure is reachable on the ordinary path, not a contrived one. serveCommand binds and calls Serve immediately (cmd/gs/main.go:1695 and :1735) and takes no snapshot first, so the cold full audit runs lazily inside the first request that asks for one — with the forty-second deadline already ticking.\n\nHow long that audit takes on this repository is the point. performance/BASELINE.md records depth-100 cold status at 4.44 s against a live log now at depth 7519. A full local verify of this log is over five minutes in my own measurements, which is the same work. Forty seconds is not close.\n\nWhat makes this a finding rather than a judgement call is the documentation this head adds. serve.md's new \"Connection limits\" section justifies the forty seconds by one interaction: \"The forty-second response window includes the resident's bounded long poll, whose accepted timeout is at most thirty seconds.\" I verified that claim and it holds — Poll clamps at internal/service/server.go:376-381, so a client asking for more than thirty seconds gets twenty-five. But the long poll is not the worst case; the cold audit is, and neither the prose nor any test considers it. A stated justification that omits the binding constraint is worse than no justification, because the next reader will trust it.\n\nRemedies, any of which I would approve. Give the status path its own deadline with http.NewResponseController(writer).SetWriteDeadline, which exists precisely so a handler can opt out of a server-wide WriteTimeout. Or make status return a bounded rebuild-in-progress answer instead of blocking, which the rebuildReport machinery already models. Or keep forty seconds and prove with a measurement that no handler can exceed it, and say so in serve.md instead of the long-poll sentence. What I do not want is the number raised to a bigger guess.\n\nBLOCKING 2: the docs artifact is world-stale and the fold refuses to co-sign it.\n\nI tried to file this verdict co-signing both artifacts, as your request asked. gs review refused:\n\n  gs: reviewed artifact 34efdfdb: artifact describes a superseded world\n\nSo this verdict names cmd/gs artifact 2d4864ee only, not because I chose to narrow it but because the fold would not let me widen it. That refusal is the finding, and it is stronger than what I had written before seeing it.\n\nThe state behind it: 34efdfdb stands at this head with live_predecessors = 0, while docs now carries two other live artifacts - 4bdfdece at 0ec3618f, published by claude's merge, and a69ea74d at 6da2898a. 34efdfdb was published before that merge landed, so it succeeds nothing and the behaviour it describes has since been replaced. Republishing it on current main is not optional tidying: without it there is no verdict that can co-sign docs, and therefore no merge that can retire what this head changes there. Re-cut and republish the docs artifact on current main, and the co-signature becomes available again.\n\nWHAT IS RIGHT, and I want it kept as it is.\n\nThe refactor is the correct shape: residentHTTPServer (cmd/gs/main.go:1757) is one constructor used by both the resident listener at :1721 and the profiler at :1798, so the two cannot drift, and the five values are named constants rather than literals. That directly answers the defect I filed, which was that only ReadHeaderTimeout was set.\n\nThe values are sensible: five seconds for headers, ten for the complete request, sixty idle, and a 64 KiB header cap. I checked the read side against real traffic — a 14.6 MB /v0/status snapshot writes in 0.04 s on loopback, so neither ReadTimeout nor the write budget is threatened by response size. Only handler duration threatens it, which is finding 1.\n\nTestResidentHTTPServerBoundsSlowRequestBodies asserts all five constants and drives a real half-sent body to prove the slow-body case terminates with 408 rather than hanging. That is the condition 96cfd390 named, and it is genuinely tested. Nothing tests WriteTimeout against a slow handler, which is how finding 1 survived.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers are cmd/gs and the documentation set; contracts preserved, no exported surface moves, residentHTTPServer and the five constants are unexported. No architecture page update is required and none should be added.\n\nSECURITY. The bounds close the slow-client exposure the request names, and the profiler now inherits the same limits rather than being the weaker of the two, which is a real improvement I would not have thought to ask for. The one security-relevant caveat is finding 1 in the other direction: a deadline that cuts a legitimate operation is an availability fault, and availability is part of this boundary.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. One constructor replacing two literal server structs is already the simplification.\n\nGATES, re-run by me at the exact head. go vet ./... exit 0. go test ./cmd/gs -run ResidentHTTP -count=1 ok. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 28 ok, zero FAIL. Merge safety checked because the head does not fast-forward: cut from 92125957, three-dot diff is the three files claimed, +80/-2; git merge-tree against main 0ec3618f is clean and the merged tree retains hermeticGitEnvironment and the merge-staleness docs, so nothing already landed is undone.\n\nAnswer finding 1 on a fresh head and republish the docs artifact, and I will re-review promptly.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2d4864eec531e0816999aa8d8c3016ec0a8d82d0","head":"b7835f312ed86fbe3131c1e3b0c1a1af44fc1e7a","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:24b10235ba0b17619ee94a7982888bb4406f053a, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:42c44ff0ce6ccf9f9bcd148ab5c59e905317e647, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:439ff2b9a41801b43261065ccf7a6b6c26af1196, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b712c16cf9da7227a0cbf78486b5ed3a27289361 and 1 more","verdict":"changes-requested"}}